WEBVTT

00:02:28.494 --> 00:02:31.688
It's always amazed me
how you can combine thinking

00:02:31.712 --> 00:02:35.106
from sociology and psychology
and philosophy and politics and economics

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:38.886
and somehow weave it
into a coherent article,

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:42.259
at least good enough for an op-ed
for the day in "The New York Times."

00:02:42.283 --> 00:02:44.364
You're just coming out with this new book,

00:02:44.388 --> 00:02:46.767
"The Second Mountain:
The Quest for a Moral Life."

00:02:46.791 --> 00:02:51.992
I'm intrigued to hear you connect
the ideas in that book, in that thinking,

00:02:52.016 --> 00:02:54.567
through to some of what
you've been writing recently

00:02:54.591 --> 00:02:57.858
about the malaise in our current politics

00:02:57.882 --> 00:03:01.377
and society and culture generally